on that day i was the assistant attorney  general in charge of the criminal  division this is before they had  homeland security department and so the  responsibility for dealing with  terrorism fell on the department of  justice and the fbi  and as i was driving into work and i had  my phone on i was talking to my deputy  he said a plane hit the world trade  center  and i thought babe was probably a small  plane pilot who had gotten confused  but as we talked a few minutes later  he said his second plane hit and then we  knew this was an attack  so almost immediately we went over to  the fbi operations center  where  the center of response was going to be  located  as we went over we heard about the plane  hitting the pentagon  and also we learned about the fourth  plane which had been hijacked  and at the fbi we spent that day and  many days afterward  trying to stop more attacks from coming  we  ran down quickly who the hijackers were  by looking at the manifests and relying  on some of the information people phoned  in from the airplanes before they  crashed and we were able to rather  quickly determine that it was probably  bin laden's people at al-qaeda  and then we tracked all the connections  through financial transactions and  communications  to see who else was out there that was  potentially a danger  who why did you think  it was bin laden because of the  connections back in 1993 when the  north tower i think had been  been targeted hadn't it by ramsay  youssef  correct it was 1993 also in the mid 90s  there was actually a plot i think called  inca to blow up airliners in the  philippines that was khalid sheikh  mohammed but as we also looked at the  names on the manifests and we began to  look at how they paid for their  uh tickets and what their communications  network was we were pretty able to trace  it back to people who were known al  qaeda members and so within a matter of  hours we had a strong  connection that we could use to make  further judgments  took less than a month  for the president to take action with  operation enduring freedom what do you  remember about that  well we knew that the attack had come  from uh al qaeda which was based they  were headquartered in afghanistan that  the taliban had refused to turn bin  laden over  and that therefore if we didn't  extract  the taliban from control over  afghanistan and if we didn't chase down  bin laden they would be using it as a  platform for further attacks and so  rather quickly we went in and we  succeeded in dislodging the taliban  and when we arrived and started to push  them back we discovered training camps  and even laboratories where al qaeda was  working on trying to develop  biological and chemical weapons  how long did you expect operation  enduring freedom to last  well i don't know that i had a clear  expectation i think we were very  successful up front initially the  challenge is always what happens after  you win what's the end game and i think  there perhaps we were quite a little bit  flat-footed and not having a clear sense  of the ability to build a cohesive  stable  and peaceful society in the placement of  the tower  and i think a lot of the problems we had  in this  succeeding 20 years were problems  establishing a governance system and a  rule of law in afghanistan  that could really lay down roots  what do you think president bush  would have thought about the decision by  president trump that was then activated  by president biden to pull out of  afghanistan in such a dramatic fashion  to coincide with the anniversary of 9  11.  well i think it was always understood or  expected that at some point the mission  would be concluded  uh i think the deal that the trump  administration struck was not a good  deal for america it was kind of a rushed  deal to find a fig leaf to cover  withdrawal and that's kind of what  president biden was stuck with when he  came into office  i do think though that we could have  done a somewhat better job in staging  our withdrawal even if it meant  reopening the deal or the negotiation  with the taliban but i think at this  point we have the opportunity  to basically say to the taliban  if you want to cooperate with us we can  find some way to be useful uh to your  effort to establish a government but if  you want to go back to the bad old days  we will come down with a lot of force  i spoke to um the former national  security adviser john bolton he was on  my breakfast program last week and he  says that in his opinion  uh the united states is in greater  danger now than it was before 9 11.  would you concur with him  i would not agree with that i mean i  think in terms of dealing with  large-scale terrorist attacks we've  built a very effective infrastructure to  prevent international terrorists from  duplicating a 911  and some of your viewers will remember  august 2006 when we worked with the  british government to disrupt the plot  to blow up a dozen airliners flying out  of heathrow airport to north america so  i think our infrastructure defense is  much better  i do think what's happened is the  internet has given  the extremists more ability to reach  into our own countries and find  sympathetic people who would then be  willing to carry out smaller scale  attacks and that's challenging because  it's not as easily detected as a  large-scale plot  additionally we're now facing domestic  terrorist issues which further create a  multiplicity of challenges for the  security community  okay mr chertoff we must leave it there  but thank you for sharing your expertise  with us today it's very much appreciated  thank you and my condolences to all
 

